<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms)</h1><p class="st-meta">310 CE – 376 CE · capital Shengle</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tuoba Xianbei principality on the Inner Mongolian frontier that the Former Qin destroyed in 376 and that was reborn ten years later as the Northern Wei.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> were a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei confederation</a> who moved into the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a>. Their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-yilu">Tuoba Yilu</a> helped the Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-kun">Liu Kun</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a>, and in return the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a> made him Duke of Dai in 310 and Prince of Dai in 315. His centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengle">Shengle</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horinger">Horinger</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dai remained a tribal confederation of herders, although Chinese advisers and captives brought farming and writing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-shiyijian">Tuoba Shiyijian</a>, who had spent years as a hostage at the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a> court, created officials, written laws and a more settled capital during his long reign from 338.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 376 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a> invaded, Shiyijian died amid family violence, and Dai was divided. After Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> collapsed, his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> restored Dai in 386 and soon renamed it the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a>, which reunified northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dai-sixteen-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms)" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_369_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>310 CE – 376 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shengle</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tuoba Yilu, Tuoba Shiyijian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Steppe shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tuoba Dai, Dai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=343&amp;focus=dai-sixteen-kingdoms" aria-label="Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7423 731 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7743 987L7855 1179M7743 987L7769 1191M7743 987L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7769 1191h0M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7743 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7743 987h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shengle. <a href="/atlas?year=343&amp;focus=dai-sixteen-kingdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
